    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a set of walnut cabinet carcasses I'm working on. I'm cutting through dovetails, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the tips of the pins every time I finish the cut. The wood is quite stable, so I don't think it's a moisture issue. Any ideas on what might be causing this or how to fix my setup?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sanding excessively can lead to splintering, particularly at the tips of the pins. Try reducing the amount of pressure and speed when sandpapering. Additionally, consider using backing tape, which can help prevent splintering while sandpapering dowels. If the problem persists, check your cutting gauge and chip removal process to ensure there is proper alignment and debris is being efficiently removed. If you find you're cutting too close or too far from the pivot point, stabilize the piece and take test cuts to understand where the error is happening before applying pressure to your main project.",
