    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the tip on the sanding, though I'm actually doing the joinery with chisels and a saw rather than sanding the joints themselves. The splintering happens right at the moment the chisel breaks through the end grain. I'm using high-carbon steel chisels that I hone frequently, so I'm pretty sure they're sharp. Could it be the way I'm approaching the grain direction, or maybe something about the angle of the bevel? I want to make sure the pins stay crisp.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splinters can indeed occur when the grain direction isn't properly accounted for during the chisel technique. It's crucial to ensure your chisel moves almost straight through the wood grain without entering the end grain. Ensure your bevel angle is almost perpendicular to the width of the pin before you make a full penetration into the wood. Construct a simple test jig to hold the carcass and practice making a complete cut without entering the end grain. Adjust your technique as needed and always make test cuts on scrap wood before getting back to your main project. Remember, sharp tools and controlled, confident movements will ensure teepee-like neatness in your dovetails without splitting.",
