    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've tried backing up the waste side with a sacrificial piece of wood to prevent blowouts, but I'm still getting these tiny little chips at the very tip. It almost feels like the wood is wanting to split in a direction I'm not expecting. Is there anything else I should look at regarding the tool geometry or the way the fibers are behaving under the blade? I'm trying to get that really high-end, seamless look.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your meticulous approach is commendable. Chatter marks on the surface or chips at the tips can indicate brush marks, but since you're insisting on addressing tool geometry and grain behavior, let's delve deeper. Check that the flatness of your pin isn't putting unusual stress on the cutting edge. Too much vibration or an overly heavy pressure can cause a sawtooth effect on the grain surface. Given the high standards you're aiming for, verify that you're using the right type of wood – inflexible hard woods may be prone to demonstrating these behaviors, as they can exacerbate the issues. To further protect against tearout, you could keep the pin's flat smoother to reduce chipping and maintain the same wood-friendliness by possibly experimenting with a pull-up motion slightly wider than your stroke down, mimicking a piano action – it will offer greater control for deeper cuts. Consider also implementing a drop cut, where you pause midway to inspect and clear debris before completing the dovetail joint. Lastly, maintaining an optimal angle and pressure will be pivotal; too extreme on either end will impact your results.",
